{"at":"2026-11-11T14:11:49.581Z","event":"workflow-result","results":[{"id":"DetectPotentialProblemsUseCase","success":true,"executed":true,"steps":["Bugbot dry-run completed after atomically completing 4 diff partitions with 1 accepted finding; no SCM mutations performed."],"payload":{"dryRun":true,"findings":[{"id":"sqlite_member_profile_schema:8:invalid-composite-parent-key","title":"Profile foreign keys reference a non-unique composite key","description":"Both profile tables reference `(household_id, member_id)` on `members`, but `members` has no unique constraint on that pair: `member_id` alone is its primary key, and the only composite unique constraint is `(household_id, ha_user_id)`. SQLite requires the referenced columns to match a primary key or unique key. With foreign-key enforcement enabled, inserting profile rows therefore fails with a foreign-key mismatch, blocking profile backfill and member initialization.","file":"backend/src/domus/infrastructure/sqlite_member_profile_schema.py","line":8,"endLine":8,"severity":"high","confidence":0.96,"category":"correctness","evidence":"The exact changed statement is in `backend/src/domus/infrastructure/sqlite_member_profile_schema.py`, source origin line 1, displayed-line offset 7, yielding line 8 (1 + 7 = 8). It references `members(household_id,member_id)`. The parent declaration in `sqlite.py` defines `member_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY` and `UNIQUE(household_id,ha_user_id)`, with no unique `(household_id,member_id)` key; the schema enables foreign keys for transactions. Consequently, profile inserts and migration backfill using this reference fail.","suggestion":"Add a unique constraint on `(household_id, member_id)` to `members`, or adjust the profile foreign keys to reference a valid unique parent key while preserving household-scope integrity.","symbol":"PROFILE_STATEMENTS","codeSnippet":"FOREIGN KEY(household_id,member_id) REFERENCES members(household_id,member_id)","fingerprint":"fp-80405d99","semanticFingerprint